9 Systematic Uncertainties for Yield and Inverse Slope Parameter (or \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\)) for \(\Delta\) and \(K^{*}\)

In order to study the systematic uncertainties on the \(dN/dy\) and \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) of \(\Delta\) and \(K^{*}\) at mid-rapidity, one needs to consider the systematic errors, which come from different sources. The sources of those errors are: (1) different fitting functions to the invariant mass spectra of \(\Delta\) and \(K^{*}\), (2) different fitting function used to fit the \(p_{T}\) spectra, (3) different residual background functions, (4) different freeze-out temperature, (5) different rapidity region considered, (6) different \(|\)Z-vertex\(|\) cut, and (7) all kinds of dynamical cuts and detector effects. Some discussions on each of these are given below.

#### Fit Functions

For extracting the yield of \(K^{*}\) within the \(p_{T}\) range 0.0 to 4.1 GeV/c, the relativistic Breit-Wigner function as shown in eqn.4.17 has been used with minimum bias d+Au events. To get the above base-line results, as mentioned earlier (Section 4.6), the mass parameter has been varied keeping the width fixed. However, one can also try to fit the minimum bias invariant mass spectra for different \(p_{T}\) bins using a simplified Breit-Wigner formula with a linear background as given by

\[f(M)=\frac{a}{2\pi\times 100}\frac{\Gamma_{0}}{(M-M_{0})^{2}+\Gamma_{0}^{2}/4}+ bM+c \tag{4.30}\]

where \(a\) is proportional to the \(K^{*}\) yield, \(M_{0}\) is the mass and \(\Gamma_{0}\) is the width of \(K^{*}\). With this, the \(K^{*}\) yield (\(dN/dy\)) and \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) have been found to change by -10.6% and +6.7% respectively from their baseline values. For the case of \(\Delta\) this study could not be carried out since the simplified BW function does no fit the invariant mass spectra obtained from data.

#### Residual Background Functions

For generating the correct invariant mass distribution of the resonances, in each case, a mixed event based combinatorial background was subtracted out of the invariant mass distribution obtained from the same-event (Section 4.4). However, there is still some residual background (RBG) present even after the subtraction of the combinatorial background. For \(\Delta\) and \(K^{*}\) the RBGs used were a Gaussian and a linear function respectively to get the baseline results. In order to study the systematic uncertainties on the \(\Delta\) and \(K^{*}\) yields, due to the above background correction, we have used both first order and second order functions for \(\Delta\) and an exponential and a second order polynomial for \(K^{*}\) to describe the residual background. The first order, second order and exponential function used are defined as follows:

\[B_{1}(M)=aM+b \tag{4.31}\]

\[B_{2}(M)=aM^{2}+bM+c \tag{4.32}\]

\[B_{3}(M)=a\times e^{-b(M-c)} \tag{4.33}\]

Using a linear background, as mentioned above, for the \(\Delta\) invariant mass spectra, the \(dN/dy\) and \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) have been found to change by -5.6% and +2.8% respectively from the baseline results obtained earlier. The second order polynomial background also resulted in systematic errors of similar order. Unfortunately, for \(K^{*}\) the exponential function did not fit the data. On the other hand use of a second order polynomial background resulted in a change of +5% and +7.9% for \(dN/dy\) and \(\langle p_{T}\rangle\) respectively.

#### Dynamical Cut Effects

There are systematic uncertainties on \(\Delta\) and \(K^{*}\) results which occur due to various dynamical cuts, like: number of fit points on the TPC tracks, the ratio of number of fit points to the maximum number of possible fit points, DCA cut on detected tracks, acceptance cut based on \(\eta\)-range, track momentum cut based on \(dE/dx\) (which contributes to maximally to the total systematic errors under various cut effects).

As discussed in Section 4.2.3, in order to reduce the amount of residual background in the invariant mass distributions, we have used the tighter p (and p\({}_{T}\)) cuts for selecting the tracks. For the case of \(\Delta\) we have selected pion tracks having p (and p\({}_{T}\)) 0.1 to 0.6 GeV/c and for proton it is 0.3 to 1.1 GeV/c for an unambiguous determination of particle identity which can be very clearly seen from Fig. 2.6. For the baseline analysis, the minimum number of fit points for pion tracks were 15 the same for proton tracks being 22. Further, all proton and pion tracks with \(|\eta|<0.8\) were selected. For \(K^{*}\), we had selected kaon tracks having p (and p\({}_{T}\)) in the range 0.2 to 0.7 GeV/c and pion tracks with p (and p\({}_{T}\)) in the range 0.2 to 10.0 GeV/c with other cuts as presented in Section 4.2.3.

To get an idea about systematic uncertainties due to some relaxation of various cuts, we have opened up the momentum range 0.1 \(<\)p (and p\({}_{T}\))\(<\) 10 GeV/c for pion and 0.3 \(<\)p(and p\({}_{T}\))\(<\) 10 GeV/c for proton. Further, tracks with DCA \(<\) 1.5 cm were selected. For studying the uncertainties in selecting the momentum cuts we have taken two sets of track momentum cuts. In the first case we take kaons tracks with 0.2 \(<\)p (and p\({}_{T}\))\(<\) 0.7 GeV/c and pion tracks with 0.2 \(<\)p(and p\({}_{T}\))\(<\) 0.7 GeV/c for constructing the invariant mass of \(K^{*}\). In the second case we take kaons tracks with 0.2 \(<\)p (and p\({}_{T}\))\(<\) 10.0 GeV/c and pion tracks with 0.2 \(<\)p (and p\({}_{T}\))\(<\) 10.0 GeV/c for the same.

